// =============================================================================
// NFTBan - Polkit Authorization for Auditors Group
// =============================================================================
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
// Purpose: Grant nftban-auditors group members read-only file access for auditing
// Location: /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/50-nftban-v030.rules
//
// Security Model:
// - Members of nftban-auditors group have READ-ONLY access to logs and reports
// - NO command execution, NO service management, NO configuration changes
// - Auditors can VIEW files in /var/log/nftban and /var/lib/nftban/reports
//
// Usage:
//   Add users to nftban-auditors group: usermod -a -G nftban-auditors username
//   Users can then read audit logs and reports without root access
//
// Access granted (READ-ONLY):
//   - /var/log/nftban/*.log (view logs)
//   - /var/lib/nftban/reports/ (view generated reports)
//   - /var/lib/nftban/snapshots/ (view state snapshots)
//
// Does NOT grant:
//   ✗ Command execution (no nftban commands)
//   ✗ Service management (no systemctl)
//   ✗ Configuration access (no /etc/nftban/)
//   ✗ Firewall access (no nft commands)
//   ✗ Write access to any files
//
// Design Philosophy:
//   ✓ VIEW-ONLY access for security auditing
//   ✓ NO execution capabilities
//   ✓ Group-based file permissions only
//   ✓ Principle of least privilege
//
// NOTE: This rule uses Unix file permissions, not Polkit command authorization
//       Directory permissions are managed via FHS (see docs)
// =============================================================================

// Auditors group uses file system permissions, not Polkit command execution
// The following directories grant group read access to nftban-auditors:
//
// /var/log/nftban/           (nftban:nftban 750, auditors can read if added to ACL)
// /var/lib/nftban/reports/   (nftban:nftban-auditors 770, auditors can read/write reports)
//
// To enable auditor access to logs, use ACL:
//   setfacl -R -m g:nftban-auditors:rx /var/log/nftban/
//   setfacl -R -d -m g:nftban-auditors:rx /var/log/nftban/
//
// This Polkit rule intentionally does NOT grant command execution privileges.
// Auditors should use standard Unix tools to view files:
//   cat /var/log/nftban/audit.log
//   less /var/lib/nftban/reports/baseline/performance.json
//   grep "ERROR" /var/log/nftban/nftban.log

polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
  // Auditors do NOT get command execution privileges via Polkit
  // They use file system permissions (group membership + ACL) for read-only access
  // This rule is a placeholder documenting the security model
  return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});

// =============================================================================
// SECURITY NOTES
// =============================================================================
//
// Why this is safe:
// - Scope limited to nftban-auditors group members only
// - Only allows execution of read-only diagnostic tools
// - No service management capabilities
// - No configuration modification capabilities
// - Standard Polkit authorization flow
//
// Allowed operations:
// - Generate system inventory reports
// - Run health checks
// - View statistics and metrics
// - Run security audits
// - Read log files via audit helpers
//
// NOT allowed:
// - Starting/stopping services
// - Modifying configurations
// - Banning/unbanning IPs
// - Updating feeds
// - Changing firewall rules
//
// Alternative approaches considered:
// 1. Grant read access to /var/log/nftban - ❌ REJECTED (exposes sensitive data)
// 2. Use sudo with specific commands - ❌ REJECTED (less flexible)
// 3. Use Polkit - ✅ CHOSEN (fine-grained control, audit trail)
//
// To verify rule is loaded:
//   pkaction --action-id org.freedesktop.policykit.exec --verbose
//
// To test authorization (as nftban-auditors group member):
//   nftban inventory        # Should work without sudo
//   nftban health check     # Should work without sudo
//   nftban stats            # Should work without sudo
//
// To add user to group:
//   sudo usermod -a -G nftban-auditors username
//   # User must log out and back in for group membership to take effect
//
// Use cases:
// - Security team members reviewing system security posture
// - Compliance auditors generating reports
// - DevOps monitoring system health without full admin access
// - External auditors needing read-only access
//
// Related:
// - 61-nftban-system.rules (for nftban system user)
// - 60-nftban.rules (for nftban group - service management)
// - 50-nftban-auth.rules (for auth helper access)
//
// =============================================================================
